{"page": 29, "text": "Part I, Item 2 of this Report should be read together with our condensed consolidated financial statements and related notes included elsewhere in this Report and our audited consolidated financial statements and related notes included in our 2025 Form 10-K. This discussion contains forward-looking statements based upon current plans, expectations and beliefs involving risks and uncertainties. Our actual results may differ materially from those anticipated in these forward-looking statements as a result of various factors, including those set forth in Part I, Item 1A \"Risk Factors\" of our 2025 Form 10-K and in Part II, Item 1A \"Risk Factors\" and other parts of this Report. We expect our operating results to fluctuate significantly from quarter-to-quarter in the future due to factors such as economic and geopolitical conditions, weather events, cost of aircraft fuel, and various other factors, many of which are outside of our control. Consequently, we believe quarter-over-quarter comparisons of our operating results may not necessarily be meaningful; you should not rely on our results for any one quarter as an indication of our future performance. Except for uncertainty related to the cost of aircraft fuel, we expect our expenses to continue to increase from wage rate cost pressures, as we acquire additional aircraft, and as our fleet ages. OVERVIEW Third Quarter 2026 Results In the third quarter of 2026, we had an operating loss of $144 million and an operating margin of (5.1)%, compared to an operating loss of $100 million and operating margin of (4.3)% in the 2025 period. The increase in operating loss was primarily driven by significantly higher fuel prices, with average fuel price per gallon for the quarter increasing 57.4% year-over-year. Operating results were also impacted by disruptions resulting from severe weather and air traffic control constraints across our core Northeast markets, which reduced completed capacity and increased crew and other operating costs. These pressures were partially offset by higher revenue driven by resilient demand and higher fares. Despite significantly higher fuel prices and operational disruptions, adjusted operating margin (1), a non-GAAP financial measure that excludes special items, improved to (3.9)% in the third quarter of 2026 from (4.1)% in the third quarter of 2025. As we progressed through the third quarter of 2026, demand remained resilient across our network despite higher fares, with strength across the booking curve, including peak and off-peak periods. Revenue growth was primarily driven by higher yields, supported in part by improvements in our revenue management technology, while customer volumes remained relatively resilient. Our third quarter 2026 highlights include the following: \u2022Third quarter 2026 system available seat miles (\"ASMs\" or \"capacity\") increased by 2.3% year-over-year. \u2022Operating revenue for the third quarter of 2026 was $2.8 billion, a 21.1% increase year-over-year.\n"}
{"page": 30, "text": "\u2022Operating expense for the third quarter of 2026 was $3.0 billion, a 22.1% increase year-over-year. \u2022Operating expense, excluding special items (1) for the third quarter of 2026 was $2.9 billion, a 20.9% increase year-over-year. \u2022Operating expense per available seat mile (\"CASM\") for the third quarter of 2026 increased by 19.4% year-over-year to 17.12 cents compared to the third quarter of 2025. \u2022Excluding fuel, special items, and operating expenses related to our non-airline businesses, our cost per available seat mile (\"CASM ex-fuel\") (1) increased by 7.0% to 11.78 cents in the third quarter of 2026 compared to the third quarter of 2025. Recent Developments JetForward JetForward, our strategic framework, is focused on four priority moves: delivering reliable and caring service, building the best East Coast leisure network, offering products and perks customers value, and providing a secure financial future. Our JetForward plan, which is designed to support our long-term profitability goals, reflects various assumptions regarding factors that may impact our operational and financial performance. For further information on potential factors that could affect the success of our strategic initiatives, including JetForward, see Part I, Item 1A \"Risk Factors\" within our 2025 Form 10-K.\n"}
{"page": 31, "text": "(1) Refer to \"Regulation G Reconciliation of Non-GAAP Financial Measures\" at the end of this section for more information on this non-GAAP measure. 24 Table of Contents PART I. FINANCIAL INFORMATION ITEM 2. MANAGEMENT'S DISCUSSION AND ANALYSIS OF FINANCIAL CONDITION AND RESULTS OF OPERATIONS The sections below highlight some actions made to support these priority moves during the quarter. Reliable and Caring Service We remain focused on delivering safe, reliable, and caring service for our customers. During the third quarter of 2026, our operations were impacted by a challenging summer operating environment, including severe weather and air traffic control constraints across our core Northeast markets, which reduced completed capacity and increased crew and other operating costs. Throughout these challenges, our crewmembers remained focused on caring for our customers and recovering our operations. We continue to work with the FAA to support longer-term improvements to the air traffic control system aimed at reducing delays. Best East Coast Leisure Network We are focused on high-performing leisure, visiting-friends-and-relatives and transcontinental routes in core geographies like New York, New England, Florida, and Puerto Rico. During the third quarter, we continued executing our strategy to build the best East Coast leisure network. As part of these efforts, we continued expanding our presence in Fort Lauderdale, where customer demand remained strong despite significant capacity growth, with third-quarter revenue per available seat mile (\"RASM\") increasing nearly 15% year-over-year on more than 65% capacity growth. We continued adding destinations and frequencies to expand travel options and connecting opportunities, with peak daily departures expected to exceed 150 in the fourth quarter of 2026. This performance reinforces our strategy of concentrating growth in markets where we see opportunities to build scale and generate attractive returns. Additionally, during the third quarter, we were selected as the successful bidder to acquire 22 operating authorizations (slots) previously operated by Spirit Airlines at New York's LaGuardia Airport. Following receipt of regulatory approval, the Company completed the acquisition in October 2026. Products and Perks Customers Value During the third quarter of 2026, we continued to enhance our products and services through the expansion of our premium offerings, loyalty program, and strategic partnerships. We also completed the transition to a new revenue management system designed to improve forecasting and optimize revenue across our network, including Mint\u00ae and connecting itineraries. We continued advancing our Blue Sky collaboration with United Airlines, providing customers with expanded travel options and reciprocal loyalty benefits. Since the partnership launched, TrueBlue\u00ae members have redeemed more than 1.1 billion points on United flights. Paisly also continued expanding its travel platform\n"}
{"page": 32, "text": "offerings, including car rentals through MileagePlus Travel. Hotels and travel insurance are expected to launch before the end of 2026, followed by flight-and-hotel packages and cruises in the first half of 2027. Our loyalty program continued to build momentum during the third quarter, with loyalty revenue increasing approximately 20% year-over-year. Following the refresh of our Premier Card, acquisitions increased more than eightfold year-over-year, while spending across our co-brand portfolio grew double digits. We also continued enhancing our premium customer experience through investments such as the opening of our BlueHouse\u2122 lounge in Boston, creating additional opportunities to deepen customer engagement. Additionally, BlueFirst\u2122, our new domestic first-class product and largest JetForward initiative, is now available for sale across more than 30 routes, with service expected to begin in December 2026 between Boston and Fort Lauderdale before expanding to additional routes. The majority of aircraft retrofits are expected to be completed by the end of 2027, with the earnings contribution from the product expected to continue building through 2028 and beyond. A Secure Financial Future To secure our financial future, we remain focused on preserving liquidity, maintaining cost discipline, and proactively managing our balance sheet. During the third quarter, elevated fuel prices and operational disruptions presented additional cost pressures. Geopolitical developments, including continued conflict in the Middle East, contributed to volatility in global energy markets and higher aircraft fuel prices, adversely affecting our operating results. Fuel prices remain subject to significant volatility based on geopolitical developments, global supply and demand, refining capacity, and other factors outside of our control. 25 Table of Contents PART I. FINANCIAL INFORMATION ITEM 2. MANAGEMENT'S DISCUSSION AND ANALYSIS OF FINANCIAL CONDITION AND RESULTS OF OPERATIONS Against this backdrop, and amid continued macroeconomic uncertainty, we remained focused on managing the factors within our control, including disciplined capacity deployment, commercial initiatives, and cost management. Our JetForward cost initiatives continue to focus on improving operational efficiency and productivity through business partner spend management, improved crew utilization, and technology modernization. Additionally, during the third quarter of 2026, the Company satisfied the applicable requirements to access the additional $250 million in financing under its existing aircraft-secured financing agreement, including delivery of the required advance notice. The financing is expected to fund in the fourth quarter of 2026, subject to the completion of customary funding mechanics. We continue to take a disciplined and proactive approach to managing our balance sheet, with a focus on maintaining liquidity and financial flexibility. We remain on track to achieve our 2026 JetForward\n"}
{"page": 33, "text": "targets and continue to focus on returning to sustained operating profitability in 2027. We also remain focused on disciplined capital investment, with annual capital expenditures expected to remain below $1 billion through the end of the decade. These priorities are intended to support improved profitability, positive free cash flow, and balance sheet strengthening over time. Liquidity At September 30, 2026, we had $2.2 billion in liquidity, which includes $1.6 billion in unrestricted cash, cash equivalents, and investment securities and our $600 million undrawn Citibank line of credit. Pratt & Whitney In July 2023, Pratt & Whitney, a division of RTX Corporation, announced the requirement, mandated by the FAA, for removal of certain engines for inspection due to a rare condition involving powdered metal used in the production of certain engine parts on the PW1100G and PW1500G engine types. These engines power our Airbus A321neo and Airbus A220 fleets. The powdered metal affects engines manufactured between October 2015 and September 2021. Those engines are now required to be inspected after they have reached a reduced number of cycles dependent on the fleet type. As a result of these required inspections and other engine durability deficiencies, as of September 30, 2026, we had two aircraft grounded due to lack of engine availability. The Company currently expects each removed engine to take approximately 170 days for the PW1500G engines and approximately 270 days for the PW1100G engines to complete a shop visit and return to a serviceable condition. We believe we are past the peak number of groundings and expect the number of aircraft on the ground due to lack of engine availability to remain in the single digits for the remainder of 2026. On July 27, 2026, we entered into supplemental support and other agreements with International Aero Engines, LLC (\"IAE\"), an affiliate of RTX Corporation, Pratt & Whitney Division, related to certain PW1100G and PW1500G engine operational disruptions, technical issues occurring through December 31, 2025 and other matters. Under the agreements, we received consideration including credits up to $105 million that may be applied toward future purchases of qualifying goods and services from IAE, IAE International Aero Engines AG and Pratt & Whitney through December 31, 2027, including in exchange for waiving certain claims. These credits are accounted for as vendor consideration under ASC 705-20 and are allocated to qualifying purchases through December 31, 2027 based on our estimated eligible spend for applicable goods and services during that period. Credits associated with operating expenditures are recognized as reductions of the related operating expenses, while credits associated with capital expenditures are recognized as reductions of the cost basis of the related assets. Embraer E190 Fleet Transition In 2025, as part of the Company's fleet transition plan, we retired our remaining Embraer E190 aircraft\n"}
{"page": 34, "text": "and entered into definitive agreements to sell the remaining E190 fleet. During the nine months ended September 30, 2026, we completed the sale of our remaining owned Embraer E190 airframes and engines and continued to sell related spare parts, and recorded a net gain of $28 million, which is included in other operating expenses in our consolidated statements of operations. We also returned our remaining leased E190 aircraft.\n26 Table of Contents PART I. FINANCIAL INFORMATION ITEM 2. MANAGEMENT'S DISCUSSION AND ANALYSIS OF FINANCIAL CONDITION AND RESULTS OF OPERATIONS RESULTS OF OPERATIONS Three Months Ended September 30, 2026 vs. 2025 Overview We reported a net loss of $257 million, operating loss of $144 million and an operating margin of (5.1)% for the three months ended September 30, 2026. This compares to a net loss of $143 million, an operating loss of $100 million and an operating margin of (4.3)% for the three months ended September 30, 2025. Our loss per share was $0.68 for the third quarter of 2026 compared to a loss per share of $0.39 for the same period in 2025. Net loss increased $114 million year-over-year primarily due to elevated fuel prices as well as higher salaries, wages and benefits, largely attributable to operational disruptions, as well as a lower current year income tax benefit. The increases in expense were partially offset by higher revenue driven by stronger demand and higher fares. Our reported results for the three months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025 included the effects of special items and certain gains on investments. Adjusting for these items, our adjusted net loss (1) was $226 million, adjusted operating loss (1) was $111 million, adjusted operating margin (1) was (3.9)%, and adjusted loss per share (1) was $0.60 for the three months ended September 30, 2026. This compares to an adjusted net loss (1) of $144 million, adjusted operating loss (1) of $95 million, adjusted operating margin (1) of (4.1)%, and adjusted loss per share (1) of $0.40 for the three months ended September 30, 2025.\nOperating Revenues\n(Revenues in millions; percent changes based on unrounded numbers) Three Months Ended September 30, Year-over-Year Change\n2026 2025 $ %\nPassenger revenue $ 2,600 $ 2,135 $ 465 21.8 %\nOther revenue 212 187 25 13.4\nTotal operating revenues $ 2,812 $ 2,322 $ 490 21.1 %\nAverage fare $ 245.11 $ 205.67 $ 39.44 19.2 %\nYield per passenger mile (cents) 18.01 14.86 3.15 21.2\nPassenger revenue per ASM (cents) 15.06 12.65 2.41 19.1\nOperating revenue per ASM (cents) 16.28 13.75 2.53 18.4\nAverage stage length (miles) 1,289 1,317 (28) (2.1)\nRevenue passengers (thousands) 10,607 10,381 226 2.2\nRevenue passenger miles (millions) 14,439 14,372 67 0.5\nAvailable seat miles (ASMs) (millions) 17,268 16,884 384 2.3\nLoad factor 83.6 % 85.1 % (1.5) pts.\n"}
{"page": 35, "text": "Passenger revenue is our primary source of revenue, which includes seat revenue and baggage fees, as well as revenue from our ancillary product offerings such as EvenMore\u00ae. Passenger revenue increased 21.8% for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to the same period in 2025. This was mainly driven by a 21.2% higher yield and a 2.2% increase in revenue passengers compared to the prior period. Other revenue increased $25 million, or 13.4%, primarily due to higher customer spend related to loyalty revenue from the non-transportation elements of the sale of TrueBlue\u00ae points. Other revenue also includes revenue from the sale of vacation packages, airport concessions, charters, advertising, and lounge revenue. We measure capacity in terms of available seat miles, which represents the number of seats available for passengers multiplied by the number of miles the seats are flown. Yield, or the average amount one passenger pays to fly one mile, is calculated by dividing passenger revenue by revenue passenger miles. We attempt to increase passenger revenue by increasing our yield and also increasing our load factor of flights, when possible. Our objective is to optimize our fare mix to increase our overall revenue per available seat mile while continuing to provide our customers with competitive fares. (1) Refer to \"Regulation G Reconciliation of Non-GAAP Financial Measures\" at the end of this section for more information on this non-GAAP measure. 27 Table of Contents PART I. FINANCIAL INFORMATION ITEM 2. MANAGEMENT'S DISCUSSION AND ANALYSIS OF FINANCIAL CONDITION AND RESULTS OF OPERATIONS Operating Expenses In detail, our operating costs per ASM, were as follows:\n(in millions; per ASM data in cents; percent changes based on unrounded numbers) Three Months Ended September 30, Year-over-Year Change Cents per ASM\n2026 2025 $ % 2026 2025 % Change\nAircraft fuel $ 868 $ 539 $ 329 61.0 % 5.03 3.19 57.5 %\nSalaries, wages and benefi